\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Shortened Parameters}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Some parameters can be specified in a shortened way now:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab -quickshowconflicts as -qsc}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab -veryquickshowconflicts as -vqsc}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab -autogamelink as -agl}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab -quickclean as -qc}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab -quickautoclean as -qac}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Changes to QuickAutoClean}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 As a fix for a problem in Dawnguard.esm (see details below in previous version) QAC was marking all records in a to be cleaned file as modified, which made xEdit assemble these records from parsed data instead of just writing out the bytes that were read when the file was loaded.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This resulted in problems when cleaning Dragonborn.esm (and possibly other files).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit is now only marking all records as modified in Dawnguard.esm}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This behavior can be controlled with 2 parameters:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab -NoAutoMarkModified prevents mark modified from being applied to Dawnguard.esm (resulting in the Soul Cairn error described below)}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab -ForceMarkModified always applies mark modified to the cleaned file (if there was anything to clean)}\par
\pard\sb360\sa180{\b\f0\fs36\ulth What's new in xEdit 4.0.2?}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Ko-Fi}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A number of users asked for a way to support xEdit development without using Patreon and/or PayPal.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is now possible to support xEdit development with both one time and monthly donations via Ko-Fi, charged directly to a credit card using stripe.com (or via PayPal) in AU$.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Ko-Fi Page is linked via the Ko-Fi button in the top right corner of the main form.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth GamerPoets Videos}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new "Videos" button has been added in the top right corner of the main form.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This will open a YouTube playlist with xEdit specific videos by GamerPoets.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Bugfixes}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab #634 - [FO4] Unknown Perk Entrypoint 157}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab #636 - Adding a script fragment to a Quest's VMAD record doesn't update the fragmentCount field}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - xEdit remembers (unwantedly) if the main form was minimized when it was last closes and restores that state}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - When the same file is saved twice within the same second, it results in an error while renaming .save files on shutdown}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - [FNV] Unused at the end of BMDT is wrongly defined as variable size (should always be 3 bytes)}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - Collapsing of record storage (used when adding/removing/sorting masters) can under certain circumstances result in an assert while trying to save the file later}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - "Deep Copy as Override" does not check records in child groups when looking for masters that need to be added to the target}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - When using Insert key in the View, it is not possible to use Ctrl to flip the "Focus newly added elements in View" option}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - [SSE] DATA in WATR is Unused}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - Without theme, the icons in the top right corner have a black background (thanks shad0wshayd3 for the fix)}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - Saving .esm or .esl files without ESM flag for SSE stripped ONAM instead of writing it as required (the game treats files with these extension always as having ESM)}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - AddMasters() can be called with names that are not valid module files}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - The navigation treeview unnecessarily checks nodes of them main game masters for changes}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - [TES5/SSE] Words of Power in SHOU should not be alignable}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - [FO3/FNV] Some strings that are translatable are not flagged as such}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - [FO3/FNV] Sort FLST should be disabled by default}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - Duplicate "Children of" GRUPs should report a non-fatal error instead of asserting}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Collapsed Conditions}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Collapsed Conditions now display the condition in an easily readable compact 1 line format.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Conditions are now collapsed by default. An option has been added to control this.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Collapse Arrays with Benign Conflict Priority by Default}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new Option "Collapse Arrays with Benign Conflict Priority by Default" has been added (defaults to true).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When this option is active, Arrays with a Conflict Priority of Benign will be initially collapsed.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Manual Cleaning functions are deprecated}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Manual Cleaning functions ('Apply Filter for Cleaning', 'Undelete and Disable References', and 'Remove "Identical to Master" records' are considered obsolete with the introduction of 'Quick Auto Clean' mode and will now only show a message explaining this.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 More information about Quick Auto Clean can be found in this What's New document (in multiple places below, use the search function).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth [Very] Quick Show Conflicts, Quick [Auto] Clean, and Auto Game Link Sub Modes}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 These sub modes of Edit mode can now be accessed using the name of the exe in addition to use of parameters.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 To use these modes, you can rename the as e.g.:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Very Quick Show Conflicts: FO3EditVeryQuickShowConflicts.exe or TES4EditVQSC.exe}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Quick Show Conflicts: SSEEditQuickShowConflicts.exe or TES5EditQSC.exe}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Quick Auto Clean: FNVEditQuickAutoClean.exe or FO4EditQAC.exe}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Quick Clean: TES4EditQuickClean.exe or SSEEditQC.exe}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Auto Game Link: FO4EditAutoGameLink.exe or FO4EditAGL.exe}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 These sub modes will now show in the Window Caption of the Main Window, to make it easy to see that xEdit has been started in one of these modes.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Auto Load for Quick [Auto] Clean}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The -autoload parameter can be used together with -quickautoclean or -quickclean parameters.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is then necessary to specify the module that should be cleaned on the command line.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 e.g.: SSEEdit.exe -quickautoclean -autoload update.esm}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Auto Exit}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The new -autoexit parameter can be used together with a number of different modes (e.g. Quick [Auto] Clean, LODGen, Script) to have xEdit automatically close after the operation is finished.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Enderal}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Initial support for Enderal has been added.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit can be renamed to EnderalEdit.exe or started with -enderal as parameter.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A known problem currently is that if Enderal was installed in one location and then later moved to a different location with Steam, Steam does not update the registry with the new installation path and xEdit will be unable to find Enderal's Data path.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Fallout 76}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Definitions have been updated to account for increased form version in 1.0.4 and 1.0.5 patches.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Sorting of INFO records}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Problems with INFO sorting have been fixed and INFO sorting is automatically enabled by default for all games where it applies. (There should normally never be a need to turn it off, and turning it off can result in files being written that will not result in the correct INFO order when read by the game engine or CK.)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Background Info: INFO records resolve their PNAM (previous INFO) reference at the moment they are loaded, for this to work correctly the referenced INFO record must have been loaded before. To achieve this, INFO records need to be sorted according to their PNAM before being written to file.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Sort INFO option has been split into "Sort INFO" (default to True) and "Fill missing PNAM" (default to False, to preserve the previous behavior were Sort INFO was turned off by default).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The navigation treeview now by default shows the INFO children of DIAL records in the order derived from their PNAM values (the same order that they are also displayed in CK and the order in which they are processed by the game) instead of sorted by FormID. This can be changed using the context menu on the FormID column header of the navigations treeview.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 New parameters have been added to control these two options (overriding whatever has been set in the options when used): -SortINFO, -NoSortINFO, -FillPNAM, and -NoFillPNAM.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth ONAMUpdate Mode}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ONAM is the signature of a subrecord in the file header that's used to list all overridden records in temporary CELL children groups in the file. It is required for ESM flagged files to allow support for loading temporary records on-demand by the game engine. xEdit already automatically correctly writes ONAM for ESM flagged files.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ONAMUpdate mode can be used to have xEdit fully automatically create ONAM subrecords also for all non ESM flagged files. The purpose is to support a new option in SSE Engine Fixes to make the game load all files as if they were masters. Further information about this can be found in SSE Engine Fixes.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 There is no reason to use ONAMUpdate mode if you aren't going to use that function in SSE Engine Fixes.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 To run xEdit in ONAMUpdate mode, you can either rename it to SSEONAMUpdate.exe or start it with the -ONAMUpdate parameter.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new Option "Always save ONAM" has been added (defaults to true). When this Option is active, xEdit will write ONAM when saving not ESM flagged files. (Without the Option, saving a non-ESM flagged file will strip out ONAM.)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When running ONAMUpdate mode, this option will be turned on and is saved in the settings file.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The option can be forced on using the -AlwaysSaveONAM parameter.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth .bsa/.ba2 Handling}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit specific code for .bsa/.ba2 has been completely replaced with the code from BSArch.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This change adds support for TES3 .bsa archives as well as support for additional texture formats for FO4 and FO76 texture .ba2 archives.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It also fixes some issues where certain .bsa files (e.g. extra voices when language in Steam has been set to Polish for TES5) could result in an endless loop while starting xEdit.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Expert Options}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Some Options have been moved to a new Experts tab which is only visible if you Know What You Are Doing.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Shrinking SpeedButtons}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 On the "UI Settings" tab, a new "Shrink SpeedButtons in the top right corner of the main form" Option has been added. The option is also available in the context menu of the buttons. (Thanks to shad0wshayd3 for improving the implementation.)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Selecting this option will remove the captions from the Help / Videos / NexusMods / GitHub / Discord / Patreon / Ko-Fi / PayPal buttons.}\par
\pard\sb360\sa180{\b\f0\fs36\ulth What's new in xEdit 4.0.1?}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Bugfixes}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab #618 - 3.3 and 4.0.0 fail to update records when changing form IDs (breaks auto-renumbering, merging)}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab #619 - Assertion failure when running REPLACE MODEL FILE NAME script}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - attempted reporting of encoding error might result in endless loop and crash}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - back/forward mouse buttons do not work in 64bit}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - Legend Form can show up with 0 size under some conditions}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - [FNV] TCFU in INFO should be called "Follow Up"}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - [TES5/SSE] "PlayerKnows" condition does not accept WOOP parameters}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - broken .bsa/.ba2 crashes the loading instead of just giving an error message}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - Remove ITM should not apply to injected records}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - "Dialog Topic" and "Worldspace" Groups can be very slow to expand}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (reported on Discord) - Script access to TSearchRec is broken in 64bit}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - the View tab is initially visible without a record having been selected in the navigation treeview}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - main records do not correctly cache that they have already searched for a child group}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - if the focused navigation treeview node is moved outside the current visible nodes, it is not scrolled into view automatically}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab (found by developer) - certain scripts with for loops or case statements might fail with Integer required error in 64 bit}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth PayPal Button}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 By popular demand, a PayPal button has been added for people that prefer to make a one-off donation instead of pledging on Patreon.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth NexusMods and GitHub buttons}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When a new update is available, the caption of the NexusMods and GitHub buttons will now be bold and underlined.}\par
\pard\sb360\sa180{\b\f0\fs36\ulth What's new in xEdit 4.0.0?}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth What's New}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A "What's New" document has been created and is embedded inside the exe file.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is automatically shown on program start. By checking the "Don't show until changed" checkbox, it is no longer shown on future starts until the What's New document has changed (because xEdit was updated).}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Table of Contents}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The TOC button in the top left corner of the What's New Form (also when docked in the What's New Tab) can be used to show or hide a Table of Contents.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Double Click or Enter in the TOC will scroll the selected header into view.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Width of the TOC is adjustable with a splitter.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 State (visible or not) and width of the TOC is saved and restored when the Form shows again.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Search}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A search edit has been added to the What's New Viewer.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ALT + S or CTRL + F focus the search edit.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 F3 when the main text is focused keeps searching from the current cursor position.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Enter when the search edit is focused keeps searching from the current position if the search text has remained unchanged, or starts for the start if the search text has changed.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Zoom}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A zoom trackbar has been added to the What's New Viewer.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Zoom can be controlled with CTRL + 0, +, or -.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth UI Themes}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit now supports a number of build in UI Themes. They can be activated either through the "Theme" sub menu in the system menu (icon on the top left corner) of the main window, or via the UI Theme tab in the Options.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The UI Theme tab in the Options provides a preview of the selected theme.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 In both the Theme menu and the UI Theme tab, the themes are separated into Light (darker text on lighter background) and Dark (lighter text on darker background) themes.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Themes require desktop composition to be enabled. The option to select themes is hidden if the system requirements are not met.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Theme awareness for conflict colour}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit is now aware of the current colour scheme (be it the standard windows colours or from an explicitly selected Theme) as Light (darker text on lighter background) and Dark (lighter text on darker background) and will automatically use modified (lighter/darker) text and background colours based on the colours specified in the Options for signaling conflict status.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Legend for conflict colours}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 At the top right corner of the View tab is now a Legend button which toggles the visibility of a Legend tool window which shows all possible combinations of Conflict This (text colour) and Conflict All (background colour).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 While the Legend form is open, when changing the focused cell in the View treeview, the matching cell (Conflict This/Conflict All) in the Legend window is focused.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth High-DPI aware}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit is now flagged as High-DPI aware and the UI should generally scale correctly when the scale factor is set to > 100% in the windows settings. A known limitation is that window caption bars and some other elements are not scaling correctly when custom themes are used.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Hamburger" Menu}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A "Hamburger Menu" button has been added to the top left corner of the main window. This button opens a menu with (currently) the following entries:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Localization}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab GameLink (if present)}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Save}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Options}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 More menu items that are unnecessarily (= not depending on context) in different context menus may get moved to this menu in the future.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth ESL support}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit now fully supports the ESL flag in the file header (as well as the .esl extension which implicitly always sets the ESL flag).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ESL flagged modules will be loaded into a "light" slot and will have their FormIDs mapped into the FExxx space. Exactly like the game engine. This means it is now possible to have up to 253 "full" modules (00 to FD) and 4096 "light" modules (FE000 to FEFFF) loaded at the same time.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The ESL flag does not affect load order (light modules can load before full modules, the first digits of the FormID are NOT a reliable way to determine load order anymore).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit will prevent files from being saved with set ESL flag if they are not compatible (contain new records with an ObjectID, that's the last digits in the FormID, > $FFF) and will warn on loading incorrectly ESL flagged modules.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ESL support can be controlled with two parameters:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -IgnoreESL will load all modules into full slots, ignoring the ESL flag and the .esl extension}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -PseudoESL will load all ESL compatible modules into light slots, even without the ESL flag and the .esl extension. This works even for games that do not have ESL support in the game engine.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Fallout 76 support}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Fallout 76 mode can be enabled by renaming the exe to FO76Edit.exe or starting it with -fo76 as parameter.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 All records have working definitions. Reference building is supported and it is possible to fully load SeventySix.esm with fully build references without errors.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Fallout 76 support is generally ready to the point that anyone should be able to use it to look at SeventySix.esm without much of a chance for error messages or crashes.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 But significant amount of work remains to further refine the record definitions and identify the many new "Unknown" values that have been added.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Referenced By Caching}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Referenced By information is now being cached after being build. The cache files only need to be rebuild if the CRC of the module or application changes.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Refs are only saved to cache if reaching a threshold of more than 500 records in the file or ref building is taking more than 2 seconds.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The message log shows if referenced information is build or loaded, and if build, if it has been saved.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 As Referenced By information is now always automatically build for the \{game\}.esm file as well, the first start after an update can take a few minutes, but all further starts will be able to read in the cached information in seconds.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The cache files have the extension .refcache and are stored by default in "Data\\\{game\} Cache\\". The actual used cache path is shown in the messages log.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The cache system can be controlled with these parameters:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -DontCache = completely disables ref caching}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -DontCacheLoad = don't load cache files if present, but save if possible}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -DontCacheSave = don't save cache files after building refs}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -C:\{path\} = path to use for cache files}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Multi-threaded building and loading of Referenced By information}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The process of building or loading Referenced By information during start is now performed in multiple threads and will scale well with the number of CPU cores available. This can significantly speed up startup time.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Application and module CRC32 information}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The CRC32 of the application file is shown at the top of the messages log}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The CRC32 of loaded modules is shown during the loading and in the navigation treeview.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Improved language and codepage support}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Module files for the supported games store their strings generally ANSI (one byte per character) strings. This requires that the correct codepage (mapping between characters and their byte values) is used. Previous versions of xEdit had very limited support for accessing module files that didn't use the same ANSI codepage as the system ANSI codepage of the local system.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit no longer simply uses the system ANSI codepage, but instead uses specific encodings for certain classes of strings.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 These changes allow explicit control of all used codepages, and make xEdit's behavior independent from the ANSI system codepage of the local OS.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth String classes}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 There are different classes of strings in regards to codepage:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab General strings - These are values like file names, developer comments, internal names that are never visible to the player.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab VMAD properties of type string or array of string - These only exist in Skyrim and later. They are strings stored in the module file, but in a distinctly different way then other strings.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Localized strings - These are strings stored in external .strings files when a module is localized.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Translatable strings - These are strings stored in the module file that a player of the game might get to see. For Oblivion, Fallout 3, and Fallout New Vegas, these are string fields explicitly marked as such in the xEdit record definitions. For Skyrim and newer, this also includes any localizable string (strings that will be stored in the external .strings files when a module is localized) if the module is not actually localized.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 General strings now always explicitly use codepage 1252 (ANSI - Latin I). This can be overridden using -cp-general:<codepage> where <codepage> is either utf8 or a codepage number. It should normally never be necessary to override this.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 VMAD properties are always explicitly stored as UTF8. There is no way to override this. All supported games always expect these strings to be UTF8 and any other codepage would result in errors.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Localized strings are accessed based on the language name that's part of the filename of the .strings files.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Languages}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For Skyrim (not SE) and older, the following language names are recognized:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab arabic - 1256}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab chinese - UTF8}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab czech - 1250}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab danish - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab english - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab finnish - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab french - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab german - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab greek - 1253}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab hungarian - 1250}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab italian - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab japanese - UTF8}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab norwegian - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab polish - 1250}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab portuguese - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab russian - 1251}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab spanish - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab swedish - 1252}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab turkish - 1254}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For SSE, the following language names are recognized:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab english - 1252}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For Fallout 4 and Fallout 76, the following language names are recognized:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab en - 1252}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Defining codepage for language in settings file}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is possible to set the codepage used for a language (new or overriding a default from the list above) by modifying the settings file (shown as "Using settings file:" in the messages log on start).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 There is currently no UI support for this, but the settings file can be opened in a text editor and a [cpoverride] section can be added or modified like this:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 [cpoverride]}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 english=utf8}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 klingon=6666}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The specified value must be utf8 or a valid codepage (which 6666 isn't, that was just an example).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The specified codepage will affect any place where a codepage is derived from language.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Default language}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit used to always default to "English" ("En" for Fallout 4) as language if not explicitly specified on the command line using -l:<language>. While it is still possible to use that switch to override the language selection, xEdit will now attempt to get the correct language from the <game>.ini file, from the iLanguage key for Oblivion, and the sLanguage key for any later games.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Localized strings}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit will automatically use the correct codepage for localized strings, even when the language is switched at runtime using the localization context menu.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The fallback for localized strings in unknown languages is always UTF8. (SSE, FO4, and FO76 always use UTF8 for anything except english.)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The codepage for these files can be overridden byte creating a text file with the same name as the .strings file, but the extension .cpoverride instead. e.g. Skyrim_English.cpoverride. The file should contain a single line with the desired codepage.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For localized strings, while this language setting affects the initial choice of which .strings files are being used for localized modules, this can be changed at runtime using the localization context menu.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Translatable strings}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Translatable string use the same codepage as localized strings, but based on the language defined at program start (either from the <game>.ini or explicitly using -l:<language>) For games before Skyrim, the same list of language names and codepages as listed above applies. Changing the language through the localization context menu after xEdit has started will *not* change the codepage being used for translatable (but not localized) strings.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The codepage used for translatable strings can be globally overridden (has priority over the language selection) using the -cp:<codepage> or -cp-trans:<codepage> command line parameter where <codepage> is either utf8 or a codepage number. -cp is supported for backward compatibility, -cp-trans is preferred.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The codepage can further be overridden on a per module basis by creating a .cpoverride file with the same name as the module, like described above for .strings files. e.g. Dragonborn.cpoverride.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Alternatively, the codepage for translatable strings can now be overridden by placing the text <cp:xxxx> somewhere in the description (in the file header) of the module. xxxx can be utf8 or any valid codepage number, e.g. 1252. codepages with less than 4 digits must use leading 0s, e.g. <cp:0930>.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Log entries}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The following entries near the top of the message log provide information about the used language and codepages:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Using language: russian}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Using general string encoding: 1252  (ANSI - Latin I)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Using translatable string encoding: 1251  (ANSI - Cyrillic)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Using VMAD string encoding: 65001 (UTF-8)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When .strings files are loaded, the used encoding is shown in the log:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 [00:00] Background Loader: [Skyrim_russian.STRINGS] Using encoding: 1251  (ANSI - Cyrillic)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When a .cpoverride is in effect, it's specifically noted:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 [Dragonborn_English.STRINGS] Using encoding (from override): 65001 (UTF-8)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When a module has a .cpoverride applied to it, it is also recorded in the log:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 [00:04] Background Loader: [Dragonborn.esm] File loaded (CRC32:A9F83BFF)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 [00:04] Background Loader: [Dragonborn.esm] Using encoding (from override): 65001 (UTF-8)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 [00:04] Background Loader: [Dragonborn.esm] Start processing}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When a module has <cp:XXXX>specified in its description, it is also recorded in the log:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 [00:12] Background Loader: [CpOverrideTest.esp] Using encoding (from File Header Description): 65001 (UTF-8)}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Load order handling}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The load order handling has been rewritten completely from scratch and should now exactly mirror the behavior of the game engine for all supported games.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Specifically, for TES4, FO3, and FNV load order is derived from file modify times.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For TES5/SSE and FO4 from the order in plugins.txt.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For TES5 (which does not contain inactive modules in its plugins.txt) the load order for inactive modules is supplemented from loadorder.txt.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For SSE and FO4, files with the .esm and .esl extensions are always treated as having the ESM flag set, even if it is missing in the file.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The load order is:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 1.\tab \{game\}.esm}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 2.\tab Update.esm - SSE}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 3.\tab Official DLC in hardcoded order - SSE and FO4}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 4.\tab \{game\}_VR.esm - SkyrimVR and Fallout 4 VR}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 5.\tab Creation Club files in hardcoded order - SSE and FO4}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 6.\tab ESM flagged files}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 7.\tab non-ESM flagged files}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If during loading of modules in this order a module is being loaded for which one or more required master module is not already loaded, then the required module is loaded first. This allows non-ESM flagged files to be pulled forward into the ESM block.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth New Module selection dialogs}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The selection dialogs for modules (used for selecting active modules at start and target modules during adding masters, "copy as ... into" and similar operations) has been completely rewritten.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is now using a treeview which allows you to see the required masters.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 There are columns for File Order (giving the exact reason for the position in the order), ESM flag, ESL flag (for SSE and FO4), Load Order (for active modules) and FormID Prefix.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The visible modules can be filtered using an edit above the treeview.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Modules with missing masters are shown with ~~strike-through~~ and can't be checked.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For selecting modules to be loaded, checked modules will implicitly check all required masters (shown with [.] instead of [X]).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 You are able to save and load named presets of checked modules.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Double Click or Ctrl+Enter will check only the selected module and automatically close the dialog with OK.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When used for "Copy as ... into", template entries allow you to specify the desired extension and initial ESM/ESL flag values for a new file.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When used for "Copy as ... into", the previous selection is remembered.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Optional 'Selection Forms require holding CTRL for double click'}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new option 'Selection Forms require holding CTRL for double click' has been added. Defaults to false.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 With this option active, CTRL must be held down for Double Click to be accepted in the Module, Mod Group, and (old style) file selection forms.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Fully implemented .ghost support}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 .ghost files are treated everywhere (except for actual file access) as if they didn't have the .ghost extension.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If a normal file and a .ghost file exist, the .ghost file is ignored.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Except in the save dialog, the UI does not show the .ghost extension.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Completely reworked ModGroups support}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ModGroups is a feature that has been in xEdit for a long time, but has gotten very little attention in the past.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This version totally overhauls the ModGroups support, adding many new features as well as providing complete UI integration for creating, editing and managing ModGroups.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A ModGroup is a way to tell xEdit that certain (non-winning) overrides of a record can be hidden (because the still visible versions account for all the changes already). By hiding away these versions they no longer participate in conflict detection which can be used to prevent false positives from showing up.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A ModGroup can never hide the winning override or the original master record.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A setup where all real conflicts have been resolved by patches and all false positives hidden by ModGroups will show an empty navigation treeview after applying a "Filter to show conflicts". With this as the starting point, adding one new mod to an existing setup with 100's of mods will result in only the conflicts caused by the addition of that new mod to show up when running "Filter to show conflicts". This makes it very easy then to adjust the load order, created targeted patches to resolve real conflicts, and finally again create new ModGroups to hide away any false positives.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth New ModGroup Selection dialog}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When the background loader is complete, a new ModGroup Selection dialog is shown. This dialog is very similar to the new Modules Selection dialog and has most of its features.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The dialog is based on a treeview, so you can directly expand the individual ModGroups to see their contents.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 You can filter the treeview to more easily find a specific ModGroup.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A system for saving and loading presets is available.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Enable/Disable and Reload ModGroups}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The context menu on the View treeview has options to disable or enable ModGroups, as well as a function for reloading all ModGroups and show the ModGroup selection dialog again.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Creating ModGroups}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 There are two ways to create ModGroups in the UI.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The first is by selecting 2 or more modules in the navigation treeview, and then using the "Create ModGroup..." function in the context menu, or pressing Ctrl+M with the navigation treeview focused. This will create a new ModGroup consisting of the previously selected modules and open it in the ModGroup Editor.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The second is when looking at the View tab with at least 3 records visible (master + 2 overrides). The context menu on the header of the view treeview has a "Create ModGroup..." function, which can also be called by pressing Ctrl+M while the View treeview is focused. This function will then show a Module Selection dialog listing all modules that contribute override records to the current View and where you can check the modules that should be part of this new ModGroup. After confirming the desired modules a new ModGroup will be created and the ModGroup Editor will be opened.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth ModGroup Editor}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The ModGroup Editor shows the list of modules that are part of the ModGroup.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 New modules can be added by pressing Insert.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Modules can be moved up or down by pressing Ctrl + Cursor Up / Cursor Down.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 CRCs can be added to a module by pressing Shift+Insert when that module is selected, or just Insert if one of the existing CRCs if that module is selected.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A selected module or CRC can be deleted by pressing Delete.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If one or more CRCs has been added to a module, then the CRC of the currently loaded module must match one of these CRCs, otherwise the module is treated as if it wasn't present.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Each module has a number of flags:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Optional - All modules NOT flagged as optional must be present for the ModGroup to be valid.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Target - Override records in this module can be hidden.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Source - A override record in this module will cause the overrides with the same FormID to be hidden from all modules flagged as Target and listed above this Source in this ModGroup.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Forbidden - If this module is loaded, the ModGroup is invalid.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Ignore LO - If not flagged, then the module must be loaded in the same order as listed in this ModGroup. There are two possible flagged values for this column:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\tx720{\f0\fs24 \endash\tab Always - The load order of the module does not matter at all.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\tx720{\f0\fs24 \endash\tab in Block - all consecutive modules with this flag form a Block. Any module above the block must be loaded before any module in the block. Any module after the block must be loaded after any module in the block. The modules inside the block can load in any order.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The flags can be toggled by focusing them and pressing space, or by clicking on them with the mouse.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Before a ModGroup can be saved, it must be given a name. There are two functions to make this easier:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Pressing Ctrl+N while having a module selected will set the name of the ModGroup to the name of the module (minus extension).}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Ctrl+C can be used on any listed module to copy its name to the clipboard which can then be pasted into the Name edit.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After confirming the ModGroup Editor with OK, a Module Selection will be shown with all modules that are part of the ModGroup to select in which .modgroups file this ModGroup should be saved.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After saving a ModGroup the "Reload ModGroups" function will be automatically triggered and the newly added ModGroup has automatically been checked.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth .modgroups files}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 .modgroups files are files with the same name as a module, but the extension .modgroups.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 These are text files that contain one or more ModGroup entries.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The exact format is described in the online help.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Only .modgroups files that have the same name as a module that is present will be loaded.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ModGroups from .modgroups files that do not belong to a currently loaded module will not show errors if they are invalid.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Editing ModGroups}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 As long as at least one ModGroup exists, a "Edit ModGroup..." function is shown in the context menu of the navigation treeview.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It first shows a ModGroup Selection dialog, then loads the selected ModGroup into the same ModGroup Editor as is used for creating new ModGroups.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Deleting ModGroups}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 As long as at least one ModGroup exists, a "Delete ModGroups..." function is shown in the context menu of the navigation treeview.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The ModGroup Selection that is then shown allows selecting one or more ModGroups for deletion.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth Updating CRCs}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 As long as at least one ModGroup exists, an "Update CRC in ModGroups..." function is shown in the context menu of the navigation treeview.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This function first shows a Module Selection with all modules that are part of ModGroups with either no CRC or missing the current CRC.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After that a ModGroup Selection is shown with all ModGroups that are missing the current CRC of one or more of the previously selected modules.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After choosing which ModGroups to update, the missing CRCs will be added to all checked ModGroups.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Compare Selected}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Compare Selected context menu entry now shows the number of currently selected records as Compare Selected (x).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Auto "Compare Selected"}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new option 'Auto "Compare Selected" when multi-selecting less than [x] records' has been added. Defaults to 5. Can be disabled by setting to a value smaller than 2.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When multi-selecting less than the specified number of records in the navigation treeview, the "Compare Selected" function is automatically triggered, showing the selected records side by side in the view tab.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When more records than the specified limit are selected, "Compare Selected" can still be triggered via the context menu on the navigation treeview, like before.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Rewritten "copy [multiple] to selected records"}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The "Copy to selected records" function (available in the context menu of the view treeview) has been rewritten.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It can now only be used when in "Compare Selected" mode. In return, it now behaves exactly the same as dragging the value from the focused cell to the other cells in the same row in the view treeview. This solves all previous problems with the function.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new "Copy multiple to selected records" functions has been added, it allows performing "Copy to selected records" for a selection from all top level entries in the current view to be performed in one operation.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Resetting settings on start}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit can be started with -resetsettings to have it automatically reset all settings.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Holding Shift+Ctrl+Alt when starting xEdit triggers a dialog asking if all settings should be reset.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 In either case, the existing settings file is being backed up and all settings are reset to their default values.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Allow direct saving}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 New command line parameters have been added that allow directly saving over an existing file (instead of saving in a temporary file and waiting until xEdit is closed to rename the file) by preventing the use of memory mapped files.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -AllowDirectSaves will load all non-official (official = game master, official dlcs, CCs) modules without using memory mapped files}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -AllowDirectSaves:a.esm,b.esl,c.esp will load only the listed modules without using memory mapped files. The list may include official modules.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Show Unsaved Hint" replaces previous "Auto Save" option}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When "Show Unsaved Hint" is checked in the Options (on by default) then if there are any unsaved changes older than 10 minutes a balloon hint is being shown under the Hamburger Menu, listing the unsaved files and how long they have been unsaved.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Clicking the hint will show the usual save selection dialog.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Clicking the [X] in the hint or ignoring it for 15 seconds until it disappears on its own will snooze it for 10 minutes.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Reset Modified flag for elements on Save}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A "Reset Modified (Bold) on Save" Option has been added (defaults to true).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 With this option active, saving will reset the modified flag for elements (which caused them to show in Bold).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Editing in view treeview}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Double Click in the view treeview now activates the in-place editor for Integer (includes FormID), Float, and Flag fields, and opens the editor form for everything else.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Showing the in-place editor for fields with large drop down lists of potential values (especially FormIDs) has been hugely (multiple orders of magnitude) sped up.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Element Editor Form}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Element Editor Form (opened by Double Click in the View treeview on string fields) now accepts Enter to insert line breaks and supports Ctrl+F to search in the text.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Apply filter}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A huge speed up in applying certain filters has been achieved.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 "Apply Filter to show Conflict Losers" has been renamed to "Apply Filter to show Conflicts" and changed from showing [ctConflictLoses] to showing [ctIdenticalToMasterWinsConflict, ctConflictWins, ctConflictLoses].}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The 3 "Apply Filter..." menu items functions have been extended with 3 "Apply Filter... (selected files only)" functions. These behave the same as the original functions, except that only selected files are added to the treeview before the filter is being applied. This will not change the filter outcome for the nodes that have been added to the treeview, and will simply prevent nodes from files that weren't included in the selection from showing up in the result. This can dramatically speed up the filter process. e.g. when applying a filter for cleaning, it's not necessary to apply that filter to any file except the one that should be cleaned.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When a filter is applied, the navigation treeview is "fossilized" (always has been that way). This means that all nodes in the treeview, visible or not, have been created at the time the filter was applied, and the treeview will not react to structural changes (like adding new records to a file) while the filter remains applied. This was not well understood by most users before. To prevent confusion, a hint is now shown above the navigation treeview explaining this while a filter is active.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When "Apply filter for Cleaning" is executed, ModGroups, "Show Master and Leafs", and "Quick Show Conflict" mode are automatically disabled.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The "By BaseRecord" check list box will now only contain records that can actually be base records (= placed in a CELL by a reference record).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When a check list box is focused, +, -, and * keys can be used to trigger "Select All", "Select None", and "Invert Selection".}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If a preset has been selected in the Filter Options dialog, and the options have been changed after loading the preset, a confirmation dialog will now be shown to confirm if these changed options should be saved back into the preset.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Reachable information}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This function has been present in xEdit for a long time, but seems to have become non-functional.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is now possible to build reachable information for all games supported by xEdit.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A record is considered "reachable" if a normal player, starting a fresh game with the current load order, and without using the console or other tricks, is able potentially to somehow interact with that record.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Once reachable information has been build, it is possible to filter for reachable/unreachable records using the usual "Apply Filter..." function.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Correct generation of reachable information requires a lot of fine tuning of the record definitions and is still a work in progress. It currently works probably best for SSE.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Editing "Master Files" in "File Header"}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Previous versions allowed to freely editing Master Files in the File Header. Because adding or removing MAST sub records directly will not renumber the FormID's stored inside the file, this would lead to behavior that is unexpected (and usually destructive in nature) for people that don't have a deep understanding of the internal workings for module files.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit will now by default prevent any direct changes to Master Files. The correct way to add a master is to use the "Add Masters" function in the context menu of the navigation treeview. The correct way to remove a master is to make sure the file no longer contains any references to the master in question and use the "Clean Masters" function in the context menu.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 To allow direct editing of the Master Files again, xEdit must now be started with -AllowMasterFilesEdit in addition to -IKnowWhatImDoing. Please only use that parameter when really required and when you actually do know what you are doing.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Improvements to adding, sorting, and cleaning masters}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 "Add Masters..." now uses the new Module Selection form.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 "Sort Masters" has been renamed to "Sort Masters (to match current load order)" to make its function more clear.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 "Clean Masters" has been renamed to "Clean Masters (= Remove all unused Masters)" to make its function more clear.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 All 3 functions should perform much faster and use much less memory. They will also no longer leave Elements that needed to be internally updated (but which don't have their visual value changed) visually marked as modified.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Optional "Show File Header Flags"}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new "Show File Header Flags" option has been added.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When this is active, the ESM, ESL, and Localized flags are shown for file nodes in the navigation treeview.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth PlayerRef [PLYR:00000014] as visible record in \{game\}.exe}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 PlayerRef (FormID 00000014) is now a visible record in the \{game\}.exe module (instead of being treated as a special hardcoded value internally).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This makes it possible to track the Referenced By information for this record.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Hardcoded.dat files updated}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Hardcoded.dat files have been updated to include more records and they are now embedded inside the exe.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is possible to override the data contained in the exe by placing a \{GameName\}.Hardcoded.Override.dat beside the exe.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Tips updated}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Tips shown at program start have been updated to take into account changes made to xEdit.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Tips no longer require the external file EditTips.txt and are now embedded in the exe.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Copy to Clipboard on navigation treeview}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Ctrl+C on the navigation treeview will copy the contents of the first column of the selected nodes to the clipboard.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Ctrl+Alt+C will copy the following value of the 3rd column.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth [Very] Quick Show Conflicts}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit can be started with the following parameters:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -quickshowconflicts - after confirming modules to be loaded, automatically load all valid mod groups and "Apply Filter to show conflicts", where any row with exactly 2 columns is always only considered as override}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -veryquickshowconflicts - same as above, but automatically loads the modules as specified in plugins.txt without showing the module selection dialog (except if CTRL is held when starting), the filter will never show the game master, and all records with less than 2 overrides are filtered without further checking conflict status}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Quick [Auto] Clean}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit can be started with the following parameters:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -quickclean - after selecting a single module, the module and required masters are loaded, a filter for cleaning is automatically applied for the selected module only, and "Undelete and Disable References" and "Remove "Identical to Master" records" are executed for the selected module.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -quickautoclean - same as above, but the "Filter, UDR, Remove ITM" sequence is repeated 3 times, automatically saving the file after each iteration, and finally "BOSS/LOOT Cleaning Report" is executed.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Quick Clean mode automatically turns off "Simple Records" (to ensure the largest number of possible ITMs are recognized) and sets "I Know What I'm Doing" mode (to prevent the edit warning).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A user reported that after cleaning Dawnguard.esm, parts of the soul cairn worldspace do no longer load in the game. This is an old issue that's already present in 3.2.1 and probably before.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 To fix this issue, xEdit is now marking all groups and main records as modified before saving when using Quick [Auto] Clean. This forces these elements to be individually saved, instead of simply writing out the bytes from the original file.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This means that using Quick Clean will now produce a different CRC than using the old manual method (explicitly applying filter and explicitly performing UDR and remove ITM).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Drag'n'Drop of whole record in view treeview}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 In the view treeview, it is now possible to copy the contents of the whole record by dragging the header (showing the file name) of one column onto another.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Mouse shortcut for "Jump To" in the view treeview}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Ctrl + Click on the view treeview header performs a "Jump To" on that record.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Nav mesh improvements}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For TES5/SSE and FO4, triangle edges with edge links are now considered equal if they resolve to the same triangle number in the same external nav mesh, even if the edge link table index stored in them is different. (Requires "Simple Records" to be turned off.) As the CK has the habit of shuffling around the order of the entries in the edge link table, even when there were no actual changes to the edge links, this will significantly increase then number of NAVM ITMs that xEdit can detect, greatly reducing the conflict surface of cleaned mods.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For TES5/SSE and FO4, comparison of vertices in triangles is now based on the vertex coordinates instead of the vertex number.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Record definitions have been improved and standardized for all games with nav meshes.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is now possible to Ctrl+Click on Triangle numbers in NAVM (both internal triangle numbers as well as edge links in triangle edges) to jump directly to the triangle in question, just like with FormIDs.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Program shutdown can be interrupted}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When the dialog for saving changed files is shown when closing the program, closing that dialog by pressing the [X] in the top right corner, will now interrupt the closing of the program, instead of behaving like the OK button has been pressed.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When an error happens during saving on program shutdown, the closing of the program is interrupted instead of simply continuing.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Compare to..."}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The "Compare to..." function now handles modules with HITMEs (FormIDs with a ModID HIgher Than Master Entries) better.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It will now remember the last folder (even across session).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Create delta patch using..."}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A "delta patch" is a module containing only the records that have changed between two versions of another module.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is, effectively, the reverse of "Merge into Master". If you have two versions of a module and produce a delta patch, then using the "Merge into Master" process of merging the delta patch into the first version of the module will result in the 2nd version of the module.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Creating a delta patch works similar to using "Compare to...". After loading the old version of a module normally, the "Create delta patch using..." function asks for a newer version of the same module and the name of the to be created delta patch.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It will then make a copy of the newer version into the data folder, using the specified name and .esu as extension (= Elder Scrolls Update, an extension that TES4Gecko introduced for delta patches).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This copied file will then be loaded using the same method as "Compare to...". Any record existing in the old version of the module and not in this new version will be copied and then flagged as deleted. Any record that is identical (using the same rules as ITM detection) between the two modules will be removed from the delta patch.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This leaves the delta patch with only the records that have changed between the two versions of the module.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is recommended that the delta patch is saved and xEdit closed after creating it.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Like with "Compare to..." last folder will be remembered (even across session).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 One of the most important uses of a delta patch is when a module that another module uses as master has been updated.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Suppose you have ModA and ModB, and a CompatPatchAB. ModA got updated and you want to figure out if CompatPatchAB must be updated.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Create a delta patch between the old and new version of ModA, then restart xEdit and load: ModA, DeltaPatchA, ModB, CompatPatchAB.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is now easily possible to go through the records in DeltaPatchA (which contains only the records that have been changed in the updated version of ModA) and see side-by-side the old values in ModA, the new values in ModA, and the values in CompatPatchAB. Wherever a value has changed between ModA and DeltaPatchA, this value can be dragged over from DeltaPatchA to CompatPatchAB.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 "Create delta patch using..." can also be used to turn an .esp replacer into a patch.esp (rename outside xEdit after creating it) which can be used on top of the original .esp.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This is especially useful in cases where for the same original .esp there are two or more .esp replacers with different functions that you normally can't use together. By changing the .esp replacers into patch.esps they can be used at the same time together with the original .esp. (May require the creation of another patch if there are conflicts between these different patches that need to be resolved.)}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth .esu as fully supported module file extension}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 .esu is now supported as file extension for modules, equivalent to .esp}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 .esu is a file extension that the game engine doesn't recognize, which makes it suitable for files like delta patches that shouldn't normally be loaded into the game.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Buttons to open Help, NexusMods, GitHub, Discord, or Patreon pages}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Buttons have been added to the top right corner of the main window to open:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Help - Online help for xEdit.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab NexusMods - The NexusMods page for xEdit, adjusted for whatever game mode xEdit is currently running as.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab GitHub - Where you can find the latest development version of xEdit, as well as report issues.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Discord - The xEdit Discord server. Real-time chat with other xEdit users and developers. Everyone is welcome.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Patreon - ElminsterAU's Patreon page for xEdit. Your support is absolutely essential to ensure ongoing future development of xEdit.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Update check}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth GitHub}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit will automatically perform a check for a newer version on GitHub}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This can be turned of with the "Don't check for GitHub update" option.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If an update is available, a balloon hint will be shown under the GitHub button on start.}\par
\pard\sb240\sa180{\b\f0\fs28\ulth NexusMods}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 xEdit will automatically perform a check for a newer version on NexusMods}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This can be turned of with the "Don't check for NexusMods update" option.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If an update is available, a balloon hint will be shown under the NexusMods button on start.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If xEdit is an even minor version (e.g. 4.0.x, 4.2.x, ...) when it is started for the first time (after this change) then update checking on GitHub is initially disabled (but can be turned on in the Options).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Patreon reminder balloon hint}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A balloon hint is shown to highlight the Patreon button.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If the button is clicked or the hint closed with the [x] then it will not be shown again for a while.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The "I'm a patron or donor" option can be used to disable the hint if you have pledged on Patreon or directly donated on NexusMods to permanently disable the hint.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Please consider supporting xEdit development. Future updates as extensive as this one directly depend on the level of support from the modding community.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Column width modes}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The Column width modes (changeable via context menu on the view treeview) have been overhauled.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 All Columns have a minimum width of half the standard width specified in Options.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Available options are now:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Standard - All columns use the initial standard width specified in Options. Column width can be adjusted and will only reset when moving to a different record}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Fit All - The title column (first column) uses the standard width, the remaining space is equally distributed among all columns. The column size is automatically adjusted if the window is resized}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Fit Text - The width of the columns is automatically adjusted to fit all text currently visible on screen. The column widths are updated automatically when scrolling.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Fit Smart - Combination of Fit Text and Fit All. The width of the columns is automatically adjusted to fit all text currently visible on screen. If the total width of all columns exceeds the client width of the treeview, then all columns except the title column (first column) are proportionally shrunk so that all columns fit inside the client width of the treeview. The column widths are updated automatically when scrolling.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Hide no conflict and empty rows}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The "Hide no conflict and empty rows" option can be enabled/disabled using the context menu of the view treeview.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When active:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab If more than one record is being displayed, all rows with a Conflict All status of Benign Conflict or lower are hidden.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab If only a single record is being displayed (which would hide all rows), empty rows are being hidden instead.}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab If Compare Selected is active, all rows with a Conflict All status of Multiple but no conflict or lower are hidden.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Option to delay updating view when scrolling through navigation treeview}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new option "Delay updating View when navigation treeview selection changes by [x] msec" has been added. Default being 50.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This prevents the View from flickering while fast scrolling through the navigation treeview (e.g. by holding the cursor up or down key) by delaying the update of the View for the specified amount of time.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Setting it to 0 disables the behavior and does instead make sure the view is updated for every record scrolled through.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Optional "Align alignable array elements"}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A new option "Align alignable array elements" has been added. Defaults to true.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When active, unsorted arrays (e.g. Conditions) will be aligned.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Alignment is not sorting!}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 As an example, without the option, a record with 2 overrides looked like this:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A  B  B}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 B  C  A}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 C}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 with alignment active, it will now look like this:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 B  B  B}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 C  C}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For arrays, their name (in the title column = first column) will have (sorted) or (aligned) added if the array is sorted or aligned.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Filter for View Treeview}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 On the View tab, above the treeview, there is now a bar with filter options.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It's possible to filter by Name and/or Value.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The filter will only keep nodes that contain the filtered for values, as well as their parents all the way to the root.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 There is an option to keep the children, siblings, and parent's siblings of nodes matching the filter.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 To change focus with the keyboard use:}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Alt+N or ? for by Name filter edit}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Atl+V for by Value filter edit}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Enter to jump: by Name -> and/or -> by Value -> View treeview}\par
\pard\fi-360\li360\tx360{\f0\fs24 \bullet\tab Cursor Down: from by Name or by Value filter edit to View treeview}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Improved cell focus handling in View treeview:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After Move Up/Down (menu) / Ctrl+Cursor Up/Down (keyboard) the focus will correctly stay with the moved element.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After Add (menu) / Insert (keyboard) the focus will be placed on the added element. This can be turned off with the option "Focus newly added elements in View" (defaults to true). The meaning of the Option can be inverted by holding CTRL while using Add / Insert.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After drop the focus will be placed on the dropped element.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Column sensitive row labels in View treeview}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The row labels (the first column) in the View treeview will adjust based on the column which currently has the focus.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This is especially relevant in combination with alignment and elements which type and name depends on the value of some other element.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Change to source for implicitly copied records during "[Deep] Copy as override/new into..."}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When performing a "[Deep] Copy as (override/new record) into..." operation of a record that is not directly contained in a top level group (e.g. a CELL inside a WRLD or a REFR inside a CELL) it is necessary that container records are implicitly copied first.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Previous versions of xEdit used the winning override of these implicitly copied records as source.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 From now on, xEdit will instead use the highest override visible to the target file (based on its masters) as a source.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Merge into master}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 To support "merge into master" functionality (for replacing the use of version control in CK) two new functions have been implemented:}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Inject Forms into master..."}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 "Inject Forms into master..." (available in the context menu of a file node in the navigation treeview) will change the FormID of all New records in the file to instead be injected records (=override records without a master record in the file where they belong according to FormID prefix).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Optionally it's possible to try and preserve the ObjectID (the not load order dependant part of the FormID) during this operation.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This does not actually yet copy the records into the master, but is a necessary first step.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Warning: The function will NOT rename any external files that might contain the FormID in their file names.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "[Deep] copy as override (with overwriting) into..."}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 "(with overwriting)" changes the behavior of the normal "[Deep] copy as override into..." function.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Normally, that function will not overwrite an existing record with the same FormID in the target.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If the "(with overwriting)" variant is used, then an existing record in the target will trigger a confirmation dialog to decide if the record should be overwritten. The dialog also gives the choice of "Yes for all" and "No for all".}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If the source record is flagged as deleted, then a confirmation dialog is shown to decide if the record should be copied (overwriting the target with a record flagged as deleted) or the existing record in the target should be removed.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 After injecting forms into master, by performing a "Deep copy as override (with overwriting) into..." from all top level groups of a module, and selecting one of its masters as the target, then selecting to overwrite all existing records and having the targets of all records flagged as deleted removed, the Version Control based functionality of CK to merge a plugin into master can be emulated.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Compact FormIDs for ESL"}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This function takes all New records with ObjectID > $FFF and attempts to change them to a free ObjectID <= $FFF. It will fail if there are too many new records in the module.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Warning: The function will NOT rename any external files that might contain the FormID in their file names.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Renumber FormIDs from..."}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This previously existing function shares its implementation with "Inject Forms into master..." and "Compact FormIDs for ESL".}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Warning: The function will NOT rename any external files that might contain the FormID in their file names.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 All 3 functions will show a list of affected record signatures before performing the renumbering.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Generalized parameters for ONAM handling}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 -FixPersistence/-NoFixPersistenceand -FilterONAM/-NoFilterONAM parameters are now generally available instead of just in tmMasterUpdate and tmESMify.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Collapsible Nodes in View tab}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Nodes in the View tab now have a [-] symbol that can be used to collapse them.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Collapsing or expanding a single node is stored in the elements and applies only for that one record. It may also get forgotten when navigating away from the record and coming back later. (This depends if the element gets freed or not, which depends on exactly which element it is and if it has been modified or not.)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When holding control while clicking the [-] in front of the node, the collapse or expansion is stored in the type definition instead of the Element and applies to all Elements of that definition. That means it will be preserved when navigating between different records.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is possible to have the type collapsed and then expand individual elements, or have the type expanded and then collapse individual elements. When expanding or collapsing a type (by holding control) then the state of all individual elements of that type is reset to match the state of the type.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 In addition to clicking on the [-], nodes can also be collapsed or expanded by double clicking on the desired row in column 0 (the title column) as long as Compare Selected is not active (which already uses double click in the title column to trigger sorting by the values in that row).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Next Object ID in File Header}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The "Next Object ID" Element in the File Header is now displayed in Hex.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When editing it, it can be set to ? to automatically set it to the highest Object ID in the Module + 1}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth File Header in View when file node selected}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The View tab will now show the File Header when the top level file node is selected in the navigation treeview. This makes it possible to scroll through the files and look at the File Header without having to expand each file node to select the File Header node.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Improved feedback during long operations}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 During long operations (building reference or reachable information, filtering, ...) the message log will now have a "still [operation]..." message added if no other message has been logged for 10 seconds and the format of the progress information in the window caption has been standardized.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This is an ongoing change and not all long operations may have been converted to the new system yet.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth -autoload command line parameter}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The -autoload parameter will not show the Module Selection dialog and just load all modules that are active according to plugins.txt. It also skips the ModGroup Selection and automatically loads all valid ModGroups.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth GameLink / FO4EditLink}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A FO4EditLink plugin for F4SE is now included.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If that plugin is loaded into FO4, and FO4Edit is started as the same time as the game, with the same load order, then it is possible to activate GameLink using the context menu of the "Path" bar at the top of the xEdit window.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Once activated, if a reference is selected in the console in game, xEdit will automatically change the View tab to showing that record.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This function is currently only available for FO4, but might be added to other games in the future.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A -autogamelink parameter has been added. It implies -autoload and will automatically activate GameLink in Reference mode at the end of the Background Loader.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth General record definition improvements and fixes}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A large number of improvements and fixes have been made to the record definitions for all supported games.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 In many cases these changes should allow previously unrecognized ITMs to be correctly recognized as such now.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A generic mechanism has been implemented to allow setting of default values in the record definitions, so that they can be automatically set when an element of that type is added. This is especially important for elements where the default of all bytes set to 0 does not result in a valid value.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A generic mechanism has been added to allow "recursive" record definitions. This now allows VMAD Properties of type "Struct" or "Array of Struct" to contain members that are again of type "Struct" or "Array of Struct", up to arbitrary numbers of nesting levels deep.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is now possible to add (VMAD) Scripts and Properties through the UI (both Adding and drag'n'drop).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Version Control Info is now decoded for all games and shows as yyyy-mm-dd User: x Index: y or None (if all 0).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Using EditorID to flag ITMs as intentional}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 In TES5/SSE and FO4/FO76 EDIT is now flagged as case sensitive, with a conflict priority of cpOverride.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 That means that changes to the case of any character will be recognized as a change. But at the same time, the EditorID will never be considered to have a higher conflict level than "override without conflict".}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 As the game engine treats EditorIDs always as case insensitive, changing the case of a character will not affect the game at all, but at the same time will make xEdit no longer treat the record as an ITM.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth File modification date/time on save}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For games where the module file modification date/time is _not_ used to control load order (TES5 and later) xEdit will no longer preserve the existing file modification date/time on save.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Adding of CELL (and ROAD) to WRLD, and reference (REFR, ACHR, ...), LAND, PGRD, and NAVM to CELL via the UI (or Add() function in scripts)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 In the UI, it is now possible to add CELL (and ROAD in TES4) to WRLD using the normal add menu.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A dialog will be shown to specify persistent or temporary + x, y}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The function will the first search for an existing CELL (copying as override into the current file if necessary) and only if no existing CELL can be found will a new one be created.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It is now also possible to add LAND, PGRD, and NAVM records to CELL through the UI.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For LAND and PGRD (each of which can only have a single record per CELL), the same search for an existing record and copy as override if necessary is implemented as for CELLs.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When adding a reference (REFR, ACHR, ...) to a temporary exterior CELL, its initial position is set to the centre of that CELL.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This functionality is also available to scripts through the existing Add() method.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Updating containing CELL of reference when necessary}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When a reference (REFR, ACHR, ...) needs to update its containing group (position changed, persistent flag changed, ...) it will now internally use the functions above to find or create the right CELL. This also fixes an existing bug where changing the position of a temporary reference did not move the reference to the correct temporary CELL (only when the record was changed to persistent and back was the cell updated, and then only if the right cell already existed, new cells were not created when necessary).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Automatic updating of navigation treeview in reaction to structural changes}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The navigation treeview will now (if not fossilised) automatically update to reflect any structural changes to a module without the need to manually collapsing and expanding the parent node.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 If a record is copied (as new or override) into a module for which the Group where the record is placed is currently expanded, it will show up instantly. (Before it was necessary to collapse and expand that group before the record showed up.)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 This change is especially noticeable together with the automatic updating of the CELL containing a reference that was mentioned above. When the position of a reference is changed in the View tab, the navigation treeview will now instantly show that reference under it's new CELL (while keeping that reference selected in the navigation treeview).}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Remove" in the header context menu of the View tab}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 The "Remove" function in the header context menu of the View tab (which had been removed in the past because it sometimes removed the whole parent group instead of just the desired record) is now fixed and available again.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth LODGen updated}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Various updates have been made to LODGen mode. Thanks sheson!}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Show floating point and decompression errors}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Messages will now be shown in the log for corrupted floating point values (showed up as NaN in the View and will continue to do so, but the message in the log is new).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Messages will now be shown when an error occurs during decompression of compressed records.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 There are known floating point and compression errors in the original Bethesda game files. The fact that these errors are shown now instead of silently ignored as before does not mean than anything is more broken then it was. You just know about it now.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Working Next Member / Previous Member for RUnion (e.g. Alias in QUST)}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 For elements where it is possible for different type of sub records to occur (e.g. ALST = Reference Alias vs. ALCS = Collection Alias vs. ALLS = Location Alias) it is now possible to switch between the different options using the Next Member and Previous Member options in the context menu of the RUnion element.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth "Apply Script..." dialog improvements}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 A Filter edit has been added above the script selection combobox. This filter edit will limit entries in the script combobox to ones that contain the filter text anywhere in their name.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When changing the selected script, if the current script has been modified, a confirmation dialog asks if the changes should be saved first. (Silently discarded changes previously).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When the dialog is closed, if the current script has been modified, a confirmation dialog asks if the changes should be saved. (Silently overwrote the existing script previously). When closing and not saving, the modified script is still the one that's executed.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 When saving a script and the file already exists, a backup copy will be made before overwriting the file.}\par
\pard\sb300\sa180{\b\f0\fs32\ulth Scripting improvements}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 procedure AddMasterIfMissing(aeFile: IwbFile; asMasterFilename: string; aSortMasters: Boolean = True); now takes an optional boolean parameter (defaults to true) to turn off automatic sorting of masters after adding}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 procedure AddMasters(aeFile: IwbFile; aMasters: TStrings); Adds the list of masters in aMasters to aeFile. If sorting is desired an explicit call to SortMasters is necessary.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 procedure BeginUpdate(aElement: IwbElement); and procedure EndUpdate(aElement: IwbElement); can be used to prevent change notifications from bubbling up the tree. This can hugely speed up creating / modifying larger arrays.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 It can be used like this:}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 var}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Properties: IwbContainer;}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 begin}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 //...}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 BeginUpdate(Properties);}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 try}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 // add man Property entries to Properties}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 finally}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 EndUpdate(Properties);}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 end;}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 end;}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 procedure wbFormIDErrorCheckLock; and procedure wbFormIDErrorCheckUnlock; can be used to disable FormID checking (e.g. to copy as override a record where the source contains unresolvable or otherwise invalid FormIDs without failing the copy).}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 Add(WorldSpace, 'CELL[P]', True) or Add(WorldSpace, 'CELL[1,5]', True) can be used to add a (or return an existing) persistent or temporary worldspace CELL to a Worldspace.}\par
\pard\sa180{\f0\fs24 ElementEditValue and ElementNativeValue now always work for Flags, no matter if the flag is currently set or not.}\par
